//! The two-opener differential oracle (FIG-3429, ADR 0099 §3).
//!
//! A durable effect-group child's replay key is its *identity*; the retained
//! request is its *authority*. A successor process may legitimately recover
//! the group — but only the retained request may run, and only through
//! runners the recovering host resolved for that request.
//!
//! This oracle stands two opener deployments up that differ on every
//! dispatch-context axis [`REBIND_FIELDS`] rules — generated by an exhaustive
//! match over the checklist, no catch-all — registers both as live opener
//! contexts in real [`ToolChildHost`]s, and drives the retained children
//! through the production path: `open_effect_group` → the group-open
//! selector → `ToolChildRunner` → `rebind_child_dispatch` → the leaf bodies.
//! Every observable a child can produce — which provider ran its body, which
//! completion client it billed, whose ledger the settlement carries, whose
//! process registry its intents landed in, whose cooperative token stopped
//! it, and whose completion authority its deferred wait was issued under —
//! is asserted to be A's, the opener that admitted it.
//!
//! The negative control does not fake a resolver: it flips the group-open
//! selector itself to its `KeyOnly` strategy (a `cfg(testing)` knob inside
//! `groups.rs`) and stages executors bound to B's live context, so the same
//! retained envelopes run under B's deployment — and every A-assertion
//! fails, which is the proof the oracle names the leak.

